  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Monday morning: An upper low that brought the colder\nairmass to the region this weekend continues to move away from the\narea toward the Great Lakes. Some clearing this evening will allow\ntemps to cool tonight mainly over the eastern zones where some\nareas could dip into the single digits. Clouds are expected to \nincrease (or at least thicken) over the western zones tonight and\nnot allow temps to be as cold. Winds will increase a bit from the\nE-SE as surface high pressure drops SE into the Dakotas.\n\nMonday afternoon through Thursday, A slow warm up and mainly dry \nconditions are expected into Thursday as a ridge slowly approaches\nfrom the west. The exception could be a brief rain or snow shower\nTuesday. Highs by Thursday should be in the mid 40s as a ridge \ncrests and exits the region, there should also be near overcast \nhigh- cirrus clouds due to the chinook arch. \n\nThursday night onward: Ensembles show slightly better chances for\nlight precipitation with an upper trough moving in Thursday night\nfollowed by a mostly NW flow. Winds look busy Friday and could\nnecessitate a Lake Wind Advisory. Temps will cool slightly \nbeginning Saturday.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nSlight deviations were made to increase NBM's Sky grids through\nthe next 6-12 hours. Otherwise, NBM looked pretty good.\n\n- High confidence(60-90%) through Monday night. \n\n- Moderate confidence(40-60%) in the forecast from Tuesday to\n  Friday.\n\n- Low confidence (15-25%) Friday night onward. \n\nTFJ",
